WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
How do you count packets when the bumps blur together?

2
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.040
You want one number: the mean quantal content, It is the average number of packets

3
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.220
one impulse releases.

4
00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.990
There are two ways to get it.

5
00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.290
They rest on different assumptions, and that is the point.

6
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:21.673
The first is direct.

7
00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:24.693
Every response is a whole number of packets.

8
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:30.663
So the average response, divided by the size of one packet, gives the average count.

9
00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:33.977
Count each failure as a response of zero.

10
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.470
This needs evoked packets the same size as miniatures, and packets that add

11
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.470
linearly.

12
00:00:40.983 --> 00:00:42.923
The second way sounds like a trick.

13
00:00:43.723 --> 00:00:44.943
It counts only the failures.

14
00:00:46.017 --> 00:00:49.557
Picture the terminal as a row of release sites, like tiny lamps.

15
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:55.410
On each impulse, every site has a small chance to flash, independently of the

16
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.410
others.

17
00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:58.593
A flash is one packet released.

18
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.417
A failure means every single site stayed dark.

19
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.550
The more packets released on average, the rarer an all-dark trial becomes.

20
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.390
Each site stays dark with chance one minus p.

21
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.610
The sites are independent, so multiply those chances together.

22
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:30.643
When p is small, that product comes very close to e^(−m).

23
00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:34.507
So the fraction of failures fixes the average count.

24
00:01:35.407 --> 00:01:36.607
The formula is on screen.

25
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:40.907
Suppose an impulse releases one packet on average.

26
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.627
How often does it release nothing at all?

27
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.107
More than a third of the time.

28
00:01:51.107 --> 00:01:52.987
The number is on screen.

29
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.270
The average is pulled up by trials that release two or three.

30
00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.870
Why bother with two methods?

31
00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:04.150
Because the failure method never uses the size of a packet.

32
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.740
If evoked packets differed in size from miniatures, the direct method would go

33
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.820
wrong. The failure method would not, as long as failures can still be told apart

34
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.820
from noise.

35
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:20.013
So when the two estimates agree, that agreement supports two things.

36
00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:25.913
That evoked and spontaneous packets are the same units, and that release behaves

37
00:02:25.913 --> 00:02:28.153
like many small, independent chances.

38
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:34.263
In a made-up recording of two hundred stimuli, the two estimates both come out near

39
00:02:34.263 --> 00:02:35.263
one packet.

40
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:40.193
The same reasoning predicts how many trials release each number of packets.

41
00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:42.533
That is the Poisson distribution.

42
00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.447
Each site's chance must be small.

43
00:02:46.607 --> 00:02:47.607
Small, not equal.

44
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.890
Now restore normal calcium.

45
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:54.410
The total release becomes large, tens of packets on every impulse.

46
00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.370
Failures vanish, so there is nothing left to count.

47
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.230
Low calcium is what makes failures common enough to measure.

48
00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:05.333
Low calcium is not just convenient.

49
00:03:06.273 --> 00:03:10.373
It makes the chances small and failures common, as the mathematics needs.
